  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why \"pure\" gold is labeled as 99.9% (or 999 fineness) rather than 100%:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udd2c True 100% Purity Is Physically Impossible\nBoth 24-karat and 999.9 fineness are generally accepted as pure gold. Despite this, in truth it is impossible to produce 100% pure gold based on current technical limits. To be truly 100% pure, a gold sample would need to consist of **every single atom** being a gold atom \u2014 something that cannot be guaranteed in practice.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \u2697\ufe0f Natural Impurities Always Exist\nPerfect purity would mean a piece has zero non-gold atoms. But nature has its own plans, and pure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling in there. These so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job \u2014 they help stabilize the metal's structure. They are a small amount, but it makes a world of difference to the end product.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83c\udfed Refining Has Technical Limits\nIt is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% (1,000 fineness). 999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence. Generally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners are able to reach in everyday production is a fineness of 999.9 gold.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udcb0 Economics of Refining\nRefining metals to remove impurities costs money. There's no benefit in making gold bullion used for monetary or jewelry means any better than 99.99%, as doing so just raises the cost input at no benefit. Beyond a certain point, the cost of achieving higher purity far outweighs any practical gain.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udccf How Purity Is Measured\nGold's purity is graded based on the **millesimal fineness scale**. This scale is used to determine the purity of a precious metal by measuring the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered fine gold. The purest gold is 999.99, also called \"five nines\" fine. Just below this is 999.9 gold, which is also called \"four nines\" fine.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nIn short, calling gold \"99.9% pure\" is not a marketing trick \u2014 it's an honest reflection of physical and chemical reality. Achieving absolute 100% purity would require removing **every single non-gold atom**, which is beyond current technological capabilities and would be extraordinarily expensive even if attempted. The tiny remaining fraction (0.1%) consists of trace elements that are nearly impossible to eliminate entirely.",
